Discard a cut body instead of keeping it: the disconnect branch unlinks the spool and reports that
nothing arrived.

This restores the old outcome for the bytes without the old exception: the route still answers
normally, but the bytes that reached the server are thrown away and the row keeps its offset, so a
resume from HEAD sends them again. The probes that must turn red are the disconnect probe (HEAD must report the bytes
that arrived), the race probe (its answer must carry the offset the concurrent append committed,
which this early return never re-reads), and the spool unit test (a caller that asked to keep a cut
body must get it). The zero-byte probe must stay green: a cut with nothing in it was already a no-op.

An idle body is cut through the same branch (MEMNG-934), so the three idle-bound probes that read
what the cut kept turn red with it — the admission-slot, kept-bytes and overtaking-retry probes —
while the idle-from-the-first-byte probe, a zero-byte cut, stays green like the disconnect one.

